WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.449 --> 00:00:07.990
MALE SPEAKER: Please
take your seats.

00:00:12.554 --> 00:00:13.970
JUSTIN BECKWITH:
Greetings, folks.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.430
How's it going?

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:17.489
Excited to be here today.

00:00:17.489 --> 00:00:20.030
We're going to be talking about
building Node.js applications

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:21.400
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.080
We're going to be
building some apps,

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:24.930
deploying them to
App Engine, talking

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:26.800
about taking those
same applications,

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.320
running them on Kubernetes
and Container Engine.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.007
We're going to use the G
Cloud NPM module to connect

00:00:32.007 --> 00:00:33.590
our app with some
of the cool services

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.800
we heard about this week, things
like the Google Cloud Vision

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.117
API, Google Cloud Storage.

00:00:39.117 --> 00:00:41.200
And then finally, if we
have some time at the end,

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.158
we're going to dig into
the way that JavaScript

00:00:43.158 --> 00:00:45.550
is helping change the face
of the internet of things.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:47.170
We've got a lot to do today.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:49.916
So before we dig in, a
little bit about myself.

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:51.040
My name is Justin Beckwith.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.020
I'm a product manager
here at Google.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.775
And before that, I was a web
developer at a bunch of places.

00:00:55.775 --> 00:00:57.150
If you want to
take a look at any

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.150
of the code we're going
to do today, all of it's

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:00.280
available on GitHub.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.500
Feel free to go check
it out, clone it.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:03.910
If you want to
ask any questions,

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:06.190
reach out to me on
Twitter @justinbeckwith.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:09.110
And for me, this
is really exciting

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:11.800
because Node.js has been a
big part of my development

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.650
experience for the
last five or six years.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:18.150
I was originally on an
engineering team back in 2010.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.565
And we kind of
stumbled upon Node.js

00:01:20.565 --> 00:01:22.940
because we were primarily a
front end focused development

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:23.790
team.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:26.150
And what was really attractive
was that finally, we

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.880
were able to take all these
years of experience we had

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.340
building front end
JavaScript web applications

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:33.600
and apply that same knowledge,
all that expertise we

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.850
had built, on the
server side as well.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.310
We didn't have to split
between front end and back end.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:40.090
We had one scheme
that homogeneously

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:41.730
could learn one
language, and then

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.272
use all of that
collective knowledge.

00:01:43.272 --> 00:01:45.521
And one of the things we
were really surprised by when

00:01:45.521 --> 00:01:48.480
we started experimenting with
it was that it without doing

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.310
very much work at all, we were
able to get great performance

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:52.577
out of our application.

00:01:52.577 --> 00:01:53.660
And you'll see that today.

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:55.590
Just kind of the way
that Node.js is built

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:59.190
makes it really easy to
build performant web apps.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:01.040
When we started
digging in, we started

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.341
using Express as our
web application server.

00:02:03.341 --> 00:02:05.090
And we were a little
bit surprised by this

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:06.750
because most of the
frameworks we'd used before

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:08.560
were part of the core
of whatever language

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.330
we were using.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:15.019
But most of the packages
out there for Node.js,

00:02:15.019 --> 00:02:16.310
they're really not in the core.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:19.450
They're provided by this third
party module system called NPM.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:20.620
And it's wildly popular.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.640
And there's just about anything
you could ever want out there

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:23.410
with it.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.670
Just last month, there were
nearly four billion downloads

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.590
of NPM packages for
authors of Node.js apps

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:32.180
that were building and
using these dependencies.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:34.410
And then over the last
six years, a lot of things

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:35.330
have changed.

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.310
This started up as
a technology where

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:38.285
maybe if you were
building a new app

00:02:38.285 --> 00:02:40.451
and you wanted to experiment,
or you were a startup,

00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:41.762
you might decide to use it.

00:02:41.762 --> 00:02:43.720
But Node.js, it's ready
for the enterprise now.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:45.720
There are real companies
taking a big bet.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.680
And with things like
the Node Foundation

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.710
and an 18-month
support cycle, it's

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:53.630
finally ready to use for
enterprise web applications.

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:55.519
So in that same
time, a lot of things

00:02:55.519 --> 00:02:57.060
have changed on
Google Cloud as well.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.815
And it's now also a great
place to run web apps.

00:02:59.815 --> 00:03:01.960
As it would turn
out, Node.js is built

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:05.320
on top of V8, the JavaScript
execution engine that's

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.110
used by Google Chrome.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.350
So we have years and
years of experience fine

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:11.737
tuning JavaScript to
run great on the client.

00:03:11.737 --> 00:03:13.320
And we're bringing
that same expertise

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.590
to helping build performant
applications on the server.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:17.030
And when you are ready
to bring them to us,

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.160
you can take advantage of
Google's infrastructure

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.050
so you're not
worried about uptime.

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:22.500
You're not worried
about scaling.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.380
We're just going to
manage all that for you.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.020
And then lastly, when you want
to extend your application

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:30.390
to use things like Cloud
ML, Vision API, BigQuery,

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.890
some of the cool stuff
we've talked about all week,

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:37.090
you can take advantage
of our APIs and services.

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:38.530
So getting started,
when you want

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:40.871
to deploy your application
to Google Cloud, really

00:03:40.871 --> 00:03:42.620
three places that
you're going to look at.

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:43.892
The first is App Engine.

00:03:43.892 --> 00:03:45.350
App Engine's been
around for while.

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:48.325
It's the most storied
part of Google Cloud.

00:03:48.325 --> 00:03:49.700
And the idea is
like I mentioned.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:51.370
You just bring your
code, deploy it to us.

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:52.010
We'll scale it.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.660
We'll manage it for you.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:56.220
If you're an organization
that's already using Docker,

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.230
Container Engine is a
great option as well.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.300
It's essentially the
Google Cloud hosted version

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:00.994
of Kubernetes.

00:04:00.994 --> 00:04:02.660
And it's a great
option for those of you

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.020
that are already into
the container revolution.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:07.380
And then finally, if you want
to have complete control,

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.850
you want to run a Windows VM,
you want to run a Linux VM,

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:11.930
and you want to kind
of own that end to end,

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:15.600
you can always create a virtual
machine on Compute Engine.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.480
So first thing I
want to do, before we

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:20.370
dig into the first demo, is we
had an announcement on Monday.

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:23.780
And Node.js on App
Engine just entered beta.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.770
So everything that
we're going to do today,

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:29.255
you're going to be able
to go home and do as well.

00:04:29.255 --> 00:04:30.710
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:31.680
Thank you.

00:04:34.534 --> 00:04:35.950
So for the first
demo, we're going

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:38.190
to build a simple web
application with Express.js,

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.364
the most popular web
framework for Node,

00:04:40.364 --> 00:04:42.530
and take a look at what
deploying that to App Engine

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:43.681
looks like.

00:04:43.681 --> 00:04:45.930
So I'm just going to use the
Express tool, nothing App

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:47.060
Engine specific here.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:51.734
And we're going to say
express node.js next.

00:04:51.734 --> 00:04:53.400
And this is just
creating an application

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:54.955
on my local machine.

00:04:54.955 --> 00:04:56.830
So I'm going to do what
the instructions tell

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:03.290
me to do here, cd into the
directory and run npm install.

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:05.620
Now, I'm installing these
dependencies locally.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:08.320
But when you deploy it into
Google Cloud into App Engine,

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.370
we're also going to run
the NPM install for you.

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:11.995
So if you have any
native dependencies,

00:05:11.995 --> 00:05:14.320
they're going to get built
on the right architecture.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.361
So just to show the standard
app running locally.

00:05:16.361 --> 00:05:19.290
We're going to do npm start.

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:21.680
Our app is up and running.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.320
And let's take a
look at this locally.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:27.810
So nothing fancy.

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:29.670
We've all probably
done this 100 times,

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:31.460
getting started
with our projects.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:33.320
So let's make a
simple change to it

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:36.190
and then deploy
it to App Engine.

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:40.260
So I'm going to cancel this
out, open up the project

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:41.510
in my editor of choice.

00:05:43.782 --> 00:05:45.490
And all we're going
to do here is instead

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:46.948
of it saying
"Express," we're going

00:05:46.948 --> 00:05:54.690
to say "Express Now
on Google App Engine."

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:55.460
All right.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:57.480
So when you're ready to
deploy your application,

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:59.960
you're going to want to go
download the Google Cloud SDK.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:02.550
That's at cloud.google.com.sdk.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:04.660
And this provides the
GCloud command line tools

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:07.760
that you can use to
orchestrate your deployments,

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:09.260
along with a lot
of the other things

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:11.950
we're going to see in the
UI in a little bit here.

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:17.107
So to deploy, I'm just going to
do gcloud preview app deploy.

00:06:17.107 --> 00:06:19.690
First thing it's going to do is
take a look at our application

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:22.600
and figure out, OK,
this is Node.js.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:25.270
Let's generate an app.yaml,
a small configuration

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:28.370
file that lets us know what type
of application you're running.

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.930
And so I'm OK with
generating that.

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:32.620
And finally, it's
going to tell us,

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:34.880
we're about to deploy our
version and we're off.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:38.310
And you see here we've got
nodejs-next.appspot.com.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:41.270
This is a project that
I'd set up earlier.

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:42.840
I'm deploying to
a specific version

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.480
because I don't want this to
replace the version I have

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:47.190
deployed out there right now.

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:48.730
And behind the
scenes, we're going

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:51.220
to be going off and creating
our app in the App Engine

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:53.030
flexible environment.

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:55.370
So this is going to take
a little bit to deploy.

00:06:55.370 --> 00:06:57.150
While that's
happening, let's take

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.460
a look at a version of
this that I've already

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:03.170
deployed out to App Engine.

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:05.540
So this is the Google
Cloud Developer console.

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:07.690
And this is the Node.js
project that we had just

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:09.270
created and deployed into.

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:11.410
The dashboard's going to
give us a good oversight

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:13.160
of the performance
of our application,

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:15.990
let us take a look at requests.

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:18.300
We can also dig into
things like latency,

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.950
traffic utilization, lots
of interesting statistics

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:22.770
available from the dashboard.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:24.190
What we're going
to take a look at

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:25.770
are the versions
of our application

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:27.480
that are actually available.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:29.250
So we just kicked
off a new deployment.

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:31.260
Every deployment
into App Engine is

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:33.600
going to create what
we call a new version.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:37.244
Now, you'll notice that when
we clicked on that link,

00:07:37.244 --> 00:07:38.660
or when we started
our deployment,

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.160
it didn't say that
it was just going

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:42.650
to nodejs-next.appspot.com.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:44.930
It had this version
number in front of it.

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:47.450
And the reason for that
is that every deployment

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:49.975
that you do in App Engine
creates a new version.

00:07:49.975 --> 00:07:51.600
And we want to give
you the opportunity

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:54.130
to go check it out,
make sure it works,

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:55.880
validate everything
with your application,

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:58.420
your deployment is working
exactly like you expected.

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:01.589
And then only when you're ready,
reroute all of your traffic

00:08:01.589 --> 00:08:02.630
over to this new version.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:04.510
Now of course, during
development time,

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:06.719
you can set it up so that
it does this automatically.

00:08:06.719 --> 00:08:08.426
But it's a really
great feature if you're

00:08:08.426 --> 00:08:10.140
building an application
that has to have

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:11.770
five nines of
reliability and you

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:13.640
want to be extra, extra
sure that you're not

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:15.604
going to deploy a bad build.

00:08:15.604 --> 00:08:17.520
But first, let's check
out the version of this

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:18.720
that I've already deployed.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:21.190
So I'm going to click
here on the version V1,

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:22.380
open it up in a new window.

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:24.200
And we have essentially
the same thing

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:28.340
that we just started the
deployment for right now.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:29.994
Now, as well as this
V1 version that's

00:08:29.994 --> 00:08:31.410
getting 100% of
my traffic, you're

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.991
going to notice we have
yellow, green, red, blue.

00:08:33.991 --> 00:08:35.990
These are just four
different versions of my app

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:38.539
that I deployed that have a
different background color.

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:40.039
Now, the reason I'm
bringing this up

00:08:40.039 --> 00:08:41.659
is because this
versions feature,

00:08:41.659 --> 00:08:43.970
it's not just useful
for validating

00:08:43.970 --> 00:08:46.840
my build before I take a new
deployment into production.

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:49.970
It's also great if I want to
do things like A/B testing.

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:52.552
What if we wanted to
know which background

00:08:52.552 --> 00:08:54.760
color of our application is
going to provide the best

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:55.900
experience for our users?

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:57.820
And we want to be able to
go measure that and use that

00:08:57.820 --> 00:08:59.278
to make decisions
about the product

00:08:59.278 --> 00:09:00.970
that we're deploying into cloud.

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:03.960
What I can do is I can
actually split the traffic

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:06.030
across those four versions.

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:08.210
So let's try doing that.

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:19.160
I'm going to take 25%
of blue, 25% of green,

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:24.120
and so on until
we get up to 100%.

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:27.550
Finally, 25% of yellow.

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:29.800
We're going to split on
cookie because otherwise, this

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:33.360
would be a difficult demo.

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:37.660
That happens sometimes, so we'll
just give it a quick reload.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:39.590
Lots of new things getting
deployed this week.

00:09:39.590 --> 00:09:41.756
Some of this stuff you're
seeing for the first time,

00:09:41.756 --> 00:09:46.690
so we're working out a
few of the kinks as we go.

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:47.300
Great.

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:47.812
So up.

00:09:47.812 --> 00:09:48.520
And look at that.

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:50.170
Our new version is
actually available.

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:52.057
So this is the version
we just deployed.

00:09:52.057 --> 00:09:53.765
Let's do our traffic
split one more time.

00:09:53.765 --> 00:09:56.860
I'm feeling good
about it this time.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:10:07.165
So we have red, 25% to blue,
25% to green, and 25% to yellow.

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:14.040
Save and-- well, we'll skip
past that at this stage.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:14.540
That's OK.

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:15.670
These things happen.

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:16.490
New code.

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:19.110
We just had to change it out
to the flexible environment.

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:21.390
But the idea is that
as I come back to this,

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:23.880
what I'm going to see now is
that every time traffic comes

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:26.855
in, one out of four users
will get red, one out of four

00:10:26.855 --> 00:10:28.940
will get yellow,
green, and so on.

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:34.290
So one of the things
you might notice up here

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:37.050
is that each of these
versions that's running

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:38.974
mentions that we
have two instances.

00:10:38.974 --> 00:10:40.890
Now, with the App Engine
flexible environment,

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:43.950
you can define how many virtual
machines your application

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:44.830
is going to run on.

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:47.002
And you can limit
that to one, two.

00:10:47.002 --> 00:10:49.460
You can tell us to auto scale
it, which it does by default.

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:51.244
Go up to 20
instances by default.

00:10:51.244 --> 00:10:52.910
But one of the things
that's interesting

00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:55.200
is that, unlike a lot
of platform as a service

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:57.830
systems you might
have used, we actually

00:10:57.830 --> 00:10:59.650
expose the underlying
infrastructure

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:01.267
that your application's
running on.

00:11:01.267 --> 00:11:02.850
So if we want to get
an idea of what's

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:05.230
happening with the V1
that's deployed over here,

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:07.330
we can click in,
and we can actually

00:11:07.330 --> 00:11:11.009
get the list of the virtual
machines that are available.

00:11:11.009 --> 00:11:13.050
So now, let's say that I
want to actually connect

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:14.500
to this and SSH.

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:18.530
You could SSH
directly into that VM.

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:20.990
Another useful thing
that you can do from here

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:23.726
is let's say that
we wanted to go

00:11:23.726 --> 00:11:25.850
and I'm going to try to do
the set traffic command.

00:11:25.850 --> 00:11:27.220
Only this time, I'm not
going to use the UI.

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:29.050
I'm going to try to
use the command line.

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:32.360
You can also use this tool we
have called the Cloud Shell.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:34.890
Cloud Shell is an integrated
development environment that

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:37.010
includes the Google Cloud SDK.

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:39.430
All the commands that I was
running locally on my terminal

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:41.350
you can run from here as well.

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:46.870
So I'm going to do gcloud
preview app services--

00:11:46.870 --> 00:11:50.535
or let's do versions list.

00:11:50.535 --> 00:11:53.035
This is going to tell us all
the versions of our application

00:11:53.035 --> 00:11:54.750
that are deployed.

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:56.750
And what I want to do
is I want to set traffic

00:11:56.750 --> 00:11:58.708
so that we're going to
route to the new version

00:11:58.708 --> 00:12:00.190
that we just went and created.

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:02.510
You can see it has
a time stamp on it.

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:12.450
So to do that, I'm going to say
gcloud preview app services.

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:14.554
And we're going to
say set traffic.

00:12:14.554 --> 00:12:16.970
And when we do that, we could,
of course, do a split here.

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:18.840
But instead, what
I'm going to do

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:27.930
is just send all of my
traffic to that version.

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:28.645
We'll say yes.

00:12:33.550 --> 00:12:35.500
And in just a moment-- good.

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:38.107
It looks like this
one actually set.

00:12:38.107 --> 00:12:39.690
So now if I come
back over here and go

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:43.490
to nodejs-next.appspot.com,
we're going to see "Express Now

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:46.300
on Google App Engine," the
version that we just deployed

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:47.374
up and running.

00:12:47.374 --> 00:12:48.790
And we were able
to do that safely

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:52.330
in a way that let us try it
before we actually sent traffic

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:54.060
to it.

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:56.810
So this was a pretty
simple application

00:12:56.810 --> 00:12:58.490
that we just made, right?

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:00.730
But let's say that we
decided we don't want to run

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:02.470
this on App Engine anymore.

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:04.485
Or we want to run it
in multiple places.

00:13:04.485 --> 00:13:06.860
What if you wanted to take
that same application you just

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:10.640
built and you want to
go run it on Kubernetes?

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:12.775
When you're ready to do
that, behind the scenes,

00:13:12.775 --> 00:13:15.400
all we're really doing with the
App Engine flexible environment

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:17.692
is creating a
Docker image for you

00:13:17.692 --> 00:13:19.150
when you do your
deployment that is

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:21.060
run on your virtual machines.

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:23.230
So you can actually
dig in and take control

00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:24.530
of the Docker container.

00:13:24.530 --> 00:13:26.770
You can customize it,
redeploy it to App Engine.

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:28.660
Or you can just
take the whole thing

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:30.852
and run it on Container
Engine, Kubernetes,

00:13:30.852 --> 00:13:33.060
your own local machine,
wherever you want to do that.

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:35.842
So let's take a look
at how this works.

00:13:35.842 --> 00:13:38.300
Right now, we create the Docker
file automatically for you.

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:39.799
But if you want to
get a hold of it,

00:13:39.799 --> 00:13:42.750
you can say gcloud preview
app gen-config--custom.

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:50.652
And what we're going to do is
generate a Docker file for you.

00:13:50.652 --> 00:13:51.610
So quick show of hands.

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:55.110
How many people here
have used Docker before?

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:55.830
So a fair number.

00:13:55.830 --> 00:13:57.130
That's actually fantastic.

00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:58.070
I was a little bit
worried I was going

00:13:58.070 --> 00:13:59.820
to start talking about
Docker and everyone

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:02.310
was going to get bored and
get in that post-lunch phase,

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:03.840
everyone taking a nap.

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:05.600
I don't want any of that.

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:08.142
So this is the Docker file that
we're using behind the scenes

00:14:08.142 --> 00:14:09.766
when we're actually
building your image

00:14:09.766 --> 00:14:11.290
and deploying your application.

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:13.000
But let's say we wanted
to do something special.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:14.416
We want to do
something different.

00:14:14.416 --> 00:14:15.559
I want to use ImageMagick.

00:14:15.559 --> 00:14:17.100
I don't know if
anyone else has tried

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:20.384
to use ImageMagick on other
PaaSes, but it's kind of hard.

00:14:20.384 --> 00:14:21.800
Or I want to do
something like use

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:24.240
FFmpeg to do video encoding.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:26.610
All you have to do is come
in, and just like you would

00:14:26.610 --> 00:14:29.090
normally do with a
Docker container,

00:14:29.090 --> 00:14:33.600
do your apt-get-- well,
first we'll do our update,

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:42.786
and then your apt-get
install imagemagick.

00:14:42.786 --> 00:14:44.410
Now, when we do this,
a standard Docker

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:45.784
build is going to
work just fine.

00:14:45.784 --> 00:14:46.570
Docker run.

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:47.500
It's going to work.

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:48.612
Deploy to Kubernetes.

00:14:48.612 --> 00:14:49.570
It's all going to work.

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:51.236
And it's the same
application that we're

00:14:51.236 --> 00:14:52.299
deploying to App Engine.

00:14:52.299 --> 00:14:53.090
There's no lock in.

00:14:53.090 --> 00:14:55.530
We're not worried about
it being vendor specific.

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:57.520
It's just going to be
portable across any

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:00.350
of-- this code will work in
any of the engines that we have

00:15:00.350 --> 00:15:02.710
and on your machine
and externally as well.

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:07.537
So a few of the things
that we just covered.

00:15:07.537 --> 00:15:09.370
The big thing I want
to drive home with this

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:13.290
is you can run your Node.js
application as it is today.

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:14.250
There's no lock in.

00:15:14.250 --> 00:15:17.250
You take the code with you,
run it wherever you want.

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:20.470
As far as NPM modules are
concerned, use any NPM module

00:15:20.470 --> 00:15:22.290
you want, within reason.

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:24.712
I happened to use Express
for this application.

00:15:24.712 --> 00:15:26.920
Hapi.js, we're going to see
an example of that later.

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:27.820
It'll work fine.

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:30.040
Jade, any of the modules
that you're using today

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:31.850
are going to work just fine.

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:33.680
We can automatically
scale this to millions

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:35.927
of requests for you.

00:15:35.927 --> 00:15:38.260
Out-of-the-box, we're going
to scale up to 20 instances.

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:40.280
And you can bump that
up as you need to.

00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:41.985
The versioned upgrades and
traffic splitting features

00:15:41.985 --> 00:15:44.000
are really great, especially
once we figure out

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:45.990
why that wasn't working.

00:15:45.990 --> 00:15:47.630
And lastly, and
most importantly,

00:15:47.630 --> 00:15:50.020
you can bring your own runtime
with the Docker support.

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:51.350
And it's not just for Node.js.

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:53.657
This comes with all the App
Engine flexible runtimes.

00:15:53.657 --> 00:15:55.615
You have complete control
over that environment

00:15:55.615 --> 00:15:56.880
and it's transparent.

00:15:56.880 --> 00:15:59.055
You can look in and see
what's happening on the VM.

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:05.760
So that was a really
simple application.

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:08.610
I mean, that was the classic
kind of hello world deployment.

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:10.610
But it's pretty rare that
our apps in production

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:12.510
are actually going
to look like that.

00:16:12.510 --> 00:16:14.990
They're typically
not just App Engine

00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:16.840
and one client hitting them.

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:19.060
Usually, they look
something like this, right?

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:21.710
Things start to get complex.

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:24.747
We have multiple modules
or multiple micro services

00:16:24.747 --> 00:16:25.580
that we're managing.

00:16:25.580 --> 00:16:28.545
We're using things like scalable
pub/sub and message queues

00:16:28.545 --> 00:16:30.280
to get messages between them.

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:31.862
We're using a
scalable file system

00:16:31.862 --> 00:16:33.445
in the cloud to store
all of our files

00:16:33.445 --> 00:16:36.160
and maybe serve them over a CDN.

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:37.220
We need databases.

00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:40.500
We need things like BigTable,
Datastore, or MongoDB,

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:43.880
or CouchDB, or
RethinkDB, whichever

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:45.400
database you're using today.

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:46.890
We need to be able to run these.

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:48.390
And usually our
architectures end up

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:49.870
looking something
a little bit more

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:53.090
like this by the
time we're done.

00:16:53.090 --> 00:16:54.650
When you're ready
to take advantage

00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:57.370
of all of these services
that we provide-- things

00:16:57.370 --> 00:16:59.860
like Cloud Storage,
Cloud Datastore,

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:04.180
BigQuery-- that's when you
use the Cloud NPM module.

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:09.460
So I should say npm install
--save gcloud and your project,

00:17:09.460 --> 00:17:12.880
it opens up access to
all the APIs and services

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:15.890
that we have on Google Cloud
so that you can expand and take

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:18.040
advantage of the other
services beyond just App

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:20.834
Engine or Container Engine
that we make available.

00:17:20.834 --> 00:17:22.702
And really, these come
in these big buckets.

00:17:22.702 --> 00:17:24.910
There's storage for things
like using your databases.

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:26.790
You can use the
databases we provide.

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:30.304
You can use MySQL with Cloud
SQL or GCS or Datastore.

00:17:30.304 --> 00:17:30.970
You can do that.

00:17:30.970 --> 00:17:34.540
Or you can bring your own
with things like Mongo DB,

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:37.207
on Compute Engine,
or on Kubernetes.

00:17:37.207 --> 00:17:39.040
We have all these cool
things with big data.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:41.890
We've got BigQuery for
accessing millions, billions

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:43.630
of records in near real time.

00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:45.710
We had some announcements
around Datalab.

00:17:45.710 --> 00:17:47.810
Pub/sub for message queuing.

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:50.570
And then you get into the
APIs, which in my opinion,

00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:54.660
those APIs, these are the real
magic of Google Cloud, right?

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:56.620
These are bits of
technology we've

00:17:56.620 --> 00:17:58.960
been incubating here
internally at Google

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:00.470
for years and years and years.

00:18:00.470 --> 00:18:02.710
And finally, we're able
to take some of these,

00:18:02.710 --> 00:18:05.550
wrap a simple API around them,
and then make them available

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:06.370
for you to use.

00:18:06.370 --> 00:18:08.505
Stuff like Cloud ML,
speech recognition.

00:18:08.505 --> 00:18:10.470
I know that's something
I've struggled with.

00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:13.160
The Vision API,
detecting if we're

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:16.290
looking at a chair or an
airplane automatically for you.

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:18.551
These are the real
magic of Google,

00:18:18.551 --> 00:18:19.550
and they're all exposed.

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:22.216
I mean, think about the types of
apps you could build with this.

00:18:22.216 --> 00:18:24.240
You could reach
billions of people.

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:26.110
You could change the
world with the types

00:18:26.110 --> 00:18:29.360
of applications you could
build with these services.

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:34.210
I used these services to
build an app about cats.

00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:37.090
That is actually my cat.

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:41.430
So I'm sure, like many of
you, I frequent Reddit.

00:18:41.430 --> 00:18:45.230
One of the Reddits
that I enjoy is r/aww.

00:18:45.230 --> 00:18:47.560
And one of the things I
noticed while I was on Reddit

00:18:47.560 --> 00:18:50.310
was that Reddit likes to talk
a lot about how they like cats.

00:18:50.310 --> 00:18:52.590
This comes up on the internet
as well as developers.

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:53.900
We're all cat friendly.

00:18:53.900 --> 00:18:57.060
Yet every time I
went to r/aww, I

00:18:57.060 --> 00:19:01.530
observed that I felt
like I saw more dogs.

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:03.130
I have a dog and a cat.

00:19:03.130 --> 00:19:06.665
And being a kind of-- I
needed answers to this, right?

00:19:06.665 --> 00:19:08.540
I didn't want to just
leave it to perception.

00:19:08.540 --> 00:19:09.970
I needed to do this analysis.

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:15.160
And I wanted to know, does
Reddit like dogs or cats more?

00:19:15.160 --> 00:19:18.260
So a casual observation was
not going to stand here.

00:19:18.260 --> 00:19:20.110
So what I did is I
built an application.

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:22.860
And what this does
is it goes, it

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:24.744
loads the first
three pages of r/aww.

00:19:24.744 --> 00:19:27.160
You can actually go look at
this right now if you want to.

00:19:27.160 --> 00:19:29.760
You're about to see
the same pictures.

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:33.450
It loads them up, sends them
into Google Cloud Storage,

00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:35.430
runs the Cloud
Vision API against it

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:37.732
to determine if it's
a dog, a cat, or both,

00:19:37.732 --> 00:19:38.690
or maybe it's a turtle.

00:19:38.690 --> 00:19:39.347
I don't know.

00:19:39.347 --> 00:19:41.710
It figures out what it is.

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:44.930
Publishes those results
back through Cloud Pub/sub,

00:19:44.930 --> 00:19:47.137
and then finally throughout
the front end module.

00:19:47.137 --> 00:19:48.470
Now you notice here I separated.

00:19:48.470 --> 00:19:50.200
We haven't talked
about modules yet.

00:19:50.200 --> 00:19:52.880
I have an App Engine front end
and an App Engine back end.

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:54.780
So I've separated the concerns.

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:56.646
You can do that inside
of one application.

00:19:56.646 --> 00:19:58.395
And that way they can
scale independently.

00:19:58.395 --> 00:20:00.769
I'm going to have a lot more
load on the back end process

00:20:00.769 --> 00:20:02.019
than on the front end process.

00:20:02.019 --> 00:20:03.977
It's important, when
you're answering questions

00:20:03.977 --> 00:20:06.600
like this, that you can scale,
because I don't want to wait.

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:08.400
I've got to know.

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:10.520
All right.

00:20:10.520 --> 00:20:15.080
So let's try it out before
I keep talking about it.

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:17.570
So this is Cloud Cats.

00:20:17.570 --> 00:20:21.580
And this is going to go do this
analysis for us in real time.

00:20:21.580 --> 00:20:23.180
So right now, we're
going to Reddit.

00:20:23.180 --> 00:20:24.264
I hope it's up.

00:20:24.264 --> 00:20:24.930
And there we go.

00:20:24.930 --> 00:20:26.010
We're starting to get
the first images in.

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:27.926
And of course, the first
one-- is that a bear,

00:20:27.926 --> 00:20:30.082
or is that actually a dog?

00:20:30.082 --> 00:20:31.540
Either way, it's
counting as a dog.

00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:32.580
That's all that matters.

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:32.845
All right.

00:20:32.845 --> 00:20:34.050
The cats are getting back in it.

00:20:34.050 --> 00:20:36.049
We've got some cute kittens
hiding behind there.

00:20:36.049 --> 00:20:38.560
That one looks like a tiger.

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:39.290
OK, it's fine.

00:20:39.290 --> 00:20:42.850
Aww, we have cuddling
dogs, funny-eared dog.

00:20:42.850 --> 00:20:45.321
But you can see dogs on the
right, cats on the left,

00:20:45.321 --> 00:20:46.820
and we're counting
them all the way.

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:49.460
And this analysis is
happening in real time

00:20:49.460 --> 00:20:52.931
with the Google Cloud
Vision API and Pub/sub.

00:20:52.931 --> 00:20:53.430
All right.

00:20:53.430 --> 00:20:56.260
I feel like I've been
seeing more dogs than cats.

00:20:56.260 --> 00:20:58.620
We've got about 75
images to process.

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:00.499
That should count
as, like, four dogs.

00:21:00.499 --> 00:21:01.040
Look at that.

00:21:01.040 --> 00:21:02.540
There are four dogs
in that picture.

00:21:02.540 --> 00:21:04.430
And look at that.

00:21:04.430 --> 00:21:08.702
My casual observation
confirmed with data.

00:21:08.702 --> 00:21:11.107
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:18.480
Organizing the world's
information, people.

00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:21.860
This is what we
do here at Google.

00:21:21.860 --> 00:21:22.366
All right.

00:21:22.366 --> 00:21:23.740
So let's take a
look at the code.

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:31.560
Or maybe not.

00:21:34.789 --> 00:21:35.455
We'll get there.

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:39.650
All right.

00:21:39.650 --> 00:21:43.490
Into cloud cats.

00:21:43.490 --> 00:21:45.055
So first thing
you'll notice here

00:21:45.055 --> 00:21:47.680
is we've got the web module and
the worker module separated out

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:50.580
as two independent
Node.js applications.

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:52.119
I'm going to dig
into the worker.

00:21:52.119 --> 00:21:54.410
First thing I want to call
out, as I mentioned earlier,

00:21:54.410 --> 00:21:56.412
this is using Hapi
instead of Express.

00:21:56.412 --> 00:21:58.745
So any web framework you want
to use-- no web framework,

00:21:58.745 --> 00:22:01.910
I hear that's popular-- you
can just use whatever you like.

00:22:01.910 --> 00:22:04.770
We have no restrictions on the
NPM modules you can import.

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:07.610
The real magic here, though, is
happening inside of vision.js.

00:22:07.610 --> 00:22:08.360
This is on GitHub.

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:09.917
You can go check this out.

00:22:09.917 --> 00:22:11.500
You can see here the
first thing we do

00:22:11.500 --> 00:22:14.070
is import the GCloud NPM module.

00:22:14.070 --> 00:22:17.496
Now, this NPM module, it has
access to all of our services.

00:22:17.496 --> 00:22:20.120
So I just happen to be using two
in this part of the code base.

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:24.222
I'm using the Vision
API and the Storage API.

00:22:24.222 --> 00:22:26.680
Now, the real interesting piece
of this code is right here.

00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:28.570
It's these four lines of code.

00:22:28.570 --> 00:22:30.230
So lines 22 to 25.

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:33.360
In those four lines of code,
we're downloading a picture

00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:34.850
from Reddit.

00:22:34.850 --> 00:22:37.400
We're saving it to
Google Cloud Storage.

00:22:37.400 --> 00:22:39.180
This right here
is using Node.js.

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:41.170
It's just using the
standard Streams API,

00:22:41.170 --> 00:22:42.956
just like you would
with FS, to pipe

00:22:42.956 --> 00:22:44.580
the image after we
download it directly

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:46.440
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:48.129
And then, once
that's done, we're

00:22:48.129 --> 00:22:49.920
going to run it through
image analysis that

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:54.000
tells us if it's a dog, a
cat, or neither, or both.

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:54.860
That's magic.

00:22:54.860 --> 00:22:56.502
That's four lines of code.

00:22:56.502 --> 00:22:58.460
We just deployed something
into a scalable file

00:22:58.460 --> 00:23:00.890
system in the cloud and
ran an image analysis of it

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:03.390
that I could never-- it would
have taken any of us years

00:23:03.390 --> 00:23:05.570
and years and years of
collective knowledge to go

00:23:05.570 --> 00:23:06.320
build.

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:09.060
And that's really the magic
that's behind this thing.

00:23:09.060 --> 00:23:12.290
So after we've evaluated all
of these images, we're then,

00:23:12.290 --> 00:23:14.720
I mentioned, publishing it
up through Cloud Pub/sub.

00:23:14.720 --> 00:23:17.220
And that's what lets our front
end nodes know that the image

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:19.020
analysis on one record is done.

00:23:19.020 --> 00:23:21.230
Let's take a quick
peek at that code.

00:23:21.230 --> 00:23:24.100
Again, using the
GCloud NPM module.

00:23:24.100 --> 00:23:28.030
And Google Cloud Pub/sub has
this concept called a topic.

00:23:28.030 --> 00:23:29.640
Think of it kind
of like a channel.

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:31.556
And the idea is that I
can publish many things

00:23:31.556 --> 00:23:33.540
into a topic, and then
consumers will all

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:36.720
create subscriptions to that
topic to read it out later.

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:38.750
You'll see this in a minute.

00:23:38.750 --> 00:23:40.000
So we're going to get a topic.

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:41.749
And then we're going
to publish our events

00:23:41.749 --> 00:23:44.052
as they get published
from the back end service.

00:23:44.052 --> 00:23:45.510
So we're going to
say, is it a dog?

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:46.380
Is it a cat?

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:46.880
Is it both?

00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:49.320
It's important to be fair.

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:51.640
And then finally, we're
going to take that data

00:23:51.640 --> 00:23:53.391
and publish it out
to the front end.

00:23:53.391 --> 00:23:55.640
So now let's take a look at
the front end web process.

00:23:58.200 --> 00:23:59.950
This one also happens
to be Hapi.js.

00:23:59.950 --> 00:24:03.167
But the real interesting code
is in here inside of Cat Relay,

00:24:03.167 --> 00:24:05.250
probably the first and
last time we'll ever create

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:08.360
a node module called Cat Relay.

00:24:08.360 --> 00:24:10.250
And here you're going
to see similar code

00:24:10.250 --> 00:24:11.920
importing the module.

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:14.010
I'm using PubNub to
send messages out

00:24:14.010 --> 00:24:15.840
to the connected web
clients, but you could

00:24:15.840 --> 00:24:17.250
use Firebase for this as well.

00:24:17.250 --> 00:24:19.870
I just happened to know PubNub.

00:24:19.870 --> 00:24:23.530
Grab the same topic,
grab the subscription.

00:24:23.530 --> 00:24:26.350
And then here's where the
real magic is happening.

00:24:26.350 --> 00:24:27.990
Get the topic, the subscription.

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:29.740
Finally, once each of
these are published,

00:24:29.740 --> 00:24:32.460
we're going to send
them out through PubNub

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:36.590
with the final message letting
us know if it's a dog or cat.

00:24:36.590 --> 00:24:39.950
Now, while I was building
this application,

00:24:39.950 --> 00:24:42.010
one of the problems
I ran into was

00:24:42.010 --> 00:24:44.930
I kept getting some data
inside of this message

00:24:44.930 --> 00:24:48.074
object that I didn't
really expect.

00:24:48.074 --> 00:24:50.490
And these kinds of things can
be hard to debug, especially

00:24:50.490 --> 00:24:52.823
when you're dealing with live
data coming from something

00:24:52.823 --> 00:24:55.230
like Reddit, where the shape
of the JSON kind of moves

00:24:55.230 --> 00:24:56.700
around a little bit.

00:24:56.700 --> 00:24:58.774
And traditionally,
debugging this locally

00:24:58.774 --> 00:25:00.690
is something that I could
do, but debugging it

00:25:00.690 --> 00:25:03.410
once you're in cloud
gets kind of hard.

00:25:03.410 --> 00:25:05.310
You have to go sifting
through all the logs.

00:25:05.310 --> 00:25:07.070
So one of the tools that
I had at my disposal

00:25:07.070 --> 00:25:08.490
that I want to
show you guys next

00:25:08.490 --> 00:25:11.900
is called Google Cloud Debugger.

00:25:11.900 --> 00:25:14.322
So check this out.

00:25:14.322 --> 00:25:16.530
I'm going to come back over
to the Developer console.

00:25:16.530 --> 00:25:18.414
We don't need this right now.

00:25:18.414 --> 00:25:20.080
And I'm going to
switch my projects over

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:21.483
to the Cloud Cats project.

00:25:25.647 --> 00:25:27.980
And then I'm going to go into
the Google Cloud Debugger.

00:25:27.980 --> 00:25:30.500
Now, I happen to have
published all of my code

00:25:30.500 --> 00:25:32.560
into Google Cloud
source repositories

00:25:32.560 --> 00:25:34.350
so that I have access
to it for debugging.

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:35.990
And you can see over
here the same code

00:25:35.990 --> 00:25:38.430
that we were looking at
on my machine is available

00:25:38.430 --> 00:25:40.594
here inside of the
Developer console.

00:25:40.594 --> 00:25:42.010
This is where it
gets really cool.

00:25:42.010 --> 00:25:45.860
I can come into Cat Relay, find
that one line of code where

00:25:45.860 --> 00:25:48.360
I wanted to know exactly what
was inside of that message

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:50.235
object that I was passing
through the PubNub,

00:25:50.235 --> 00:25:52.530
and we can set a breakpoint.

00:25:52.530 --> 00:25:54.970
Now, this breakpoint, it's
not a traditional debugger

00:25:54.970 --> 00:25:57.870
breakpoint, if you've used
Eclipse or Visual Studio.

00:25:57.870 --> 00:25:59.400
It's a passive debugger.

00:25:59.400 --> 00:26:00.820
What this means
is that people can

00:26:00.820 --> 00:26:03.540
be running against our
application in production,

00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:07.290
and we're able to capture call
stack and variable information

00:26:07.290 --> 00:26:10.810
without affecting performance,
without stopping execution.

00:26:10.810 --> 00:26:12.530
So we can actually
come back over here.

00:26:12.530 --> 00:26:14.880
I can start to run the
simulation again, get a few

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:16.559
more pages of data.

00:26:16.559 --> 00:26:18.350
And as soon as it hits
this-- look at that.

00:26:18.350 --> 00:26:19.570
It just hit it.

00:26:19.570 --> 00:26:22.970
That code got hit in real
time inside of App Engine.

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:26.010
I'm able to go in, take
a look at the call stack.

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:28.340
And most importantly,
I can come and debug

00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:30.910
the variables that are
in the local scope.

00:26:30.910 --> 00:26:32.780
So I can see, OK, this message.

00:26:32.780 --> 00:26:34.350
I want to know what
data is in there.

00:26:34.350 --> 00:26:35.520
Here's the ID.

00:26:35.520 --> 00:26:37.560
Here's the data object.

00:26:37.560 --> 00:26:40.340
Here's the URL that is
going out to Reddit.

00:26:40.340 --> 00:26:42.070
And the type wasn't dog or cat.

00:26:42.070 --> 00:26:43.730
It was other.

00:26:43.730 --> 00:26:45.885
So this is a pretty
incredible experience

00:26:45.885 --> 00:26:47.635
if you've ever had a
problem in production

00:26:47.635 --> 00:26:49.447
that you needed to debug.

00:26:49.447 --> 00:26:51.030
Another useful thing
we have out there

00:26:51.030 --> 00:26:52.830
for debugging production issues.

00:26:52.830 --> 00:26:56.090
Let's say you're noticing
a decrease in performance.

00:26:56.090 --> 00:26:59.600
We can also use
Google Cloud Trace.

00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:03.060
Now, what Trace does is it
measures the amount of time

00:27:03.060 --> 00:27:06.200
during my HTTP request
and breaks it down

00:27:06.200 --> 00:27:08.080
into each other network
request that I'm

00:27:08.080 --> 00:27:10.200
making during the initial one.

00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:11.840
So for something
like Cloud Cats,

00:27:11.840 --> 00:27:14.090
we were talking to Reddit,
we were downloading images,

00:27:14.090 --> 00:27:16.130
we were uploading them
to Google Cloud Storage,

00:27:16.130 --> 00:27:18.260
we were calling APIs,
we were using Pub/sub.

00:27:18.260 --> 00:27:21.312
We had network requests
all over the place.

00:27:21.312 --> 00:27:22.270
I want to measure them.

00:27:22.270 --> 00:27:24.540
I want to know, what's
the long pole in the tent?

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:25.680
What's making it slow?

00:27:25.680 --> 00:27:27.640
How do I debug that?

00:27:27.640 --> 00:27:29.580
And using Cloud
Trace, I can do that.

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:32.800
Here you can see there's our
sample we ran one minute ago.

00:27:32.800 --> 00:27:35.060
The Go endpoint is the
one that I was using

00:27:35.060 --> 00:27:38.020
to measure all this stuff.

00:27:38.020 --> 00:27:39.330
And here we have it.

00:27:39.330 --> 00:27:40.860
So the request was
10 seconds long.

00:27:40.860 --> 00:27:43.980
It was doing a lot of stuff,
so I don't feel bad about that.

00:27:43.980 --> 00:27:46.500
We made first request to
Reddit, second request.

00:27:46.500 --> 00:27:48.110
I asked for three pages of data.

00:27:48.110 --> 00:27:50.026
They have to be serial
because of the way they

00:27:50.026 --> 00:27:51.720
do their next token on it.

00:27:51.720 --> 00:27:55.200
And then in parallel, we
go download all the images

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:57.020
off Reddit Media.

00:27:57.020 --> 00:27:58.870
We upload them all to
Google Cloud Storage.

00:27:58.870 --> 00:28:01.270
You can see this
going into the bucket.

00:28:01.270 --> 00:28:04.510
And then you can see us
starting to analyze them

00:28:04.510 --> 00:28:05.377
with the Vision API.

00:28:05.377 --> 00:28:07.002
So it turns out in
this case, the thing

00:28:07.002 --> 00:28:09.680
that was taking a long time
was actually calling the API.

00:28:09.680 --> 00:28:11.740
But let's say that
we found something

00:28:11.740 --> 00:28:14.370
where we needed to parallelize
something that was serial.

00:28:14.370 --> 00:28:16.490
This is a great tool
to tell us what's

00:28:16.490 --> 00:28:19.490
taking time during our
requests for our applications

00:28:19.490 --> 00:28:22.800
with barely a performance
hit in production.

00:28:22.800 --> 00:28:26.730
So when we're building our
applications for the cloud

00:28:26.730 --> 00:28:30.630
and we're kind of converting
from something that we have

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:33.240
full control over, we
need to really understand

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:34.682
how it's performing.

00:28:34.682 --> 00:28:35.890
We need these types of tools.

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:36.700
We need Cloud Debugger.

00:28:36.700 --> 00:28:37.600
We need Trace tools.

00:28:37.600 --> 00:28:39.770
We need better logging.

00:28:39.770 --> 00:28:41.980
And when it comes to
building Node.js applications

00:28:41.980 --> 00:28:44.220
in the enterprise,
there's no one better

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:47.935
to talk to than Joe McCann,
CEO, cofounder of NodeSource.

00:28:47.935 --> 00:28:54.800
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:54.800 --> 00:28:56.770
JOE MCCANN: Thanks, Justin.

00:28:56.770 --> 00:28:59.600
There's nothing like talking
about enterprise application

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:01.780
development right
after a cat demo.

00:29:01.780 --> 00:29:04.240
So thanks for that.

00:29:04.240 --> 00:29:06.660
So I'm Joe McCann and
I work at NodeSource.

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:08.130
Who is NodeSource?

00:29:08.130 --> 00:29:13.300
We are the enterprise Node.js
company currently offering

00:29:13.300 --> 00:29:17.760
the only commercial version
of Node.js explicitly targeted

00:29:17.760 --> 00:29:20.870
at the enterprise
use cases of Node.

00:29:20.870 --> 00:29:23.280
So a rough analogy
is kind think of us

00:29:23.280 --> 00:29:27.230
as what RedHat does for Linux,
we kind of do that for Node.js.

00:29:27.230 --> 00:29:29.920
But how did we even
earn sort of the right

00:29:29.920 --> 00:29:33.390
to have a commercial
version of Node.js?

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:36.040
Well, at NodeSource,
we love Node.

00:29:36.040 --> 00:29:38.810
In fact, we have the
most core commiters,

00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:42.120
contributors to the open
source project itself,

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:45.640
including the Node.js
lead, Rod Vagg,

00:29:45.640 --> 00:29:47.850
who also represents
the Technical Steering

00:29:47.850 --> 00:29:51.690
Committee on the Node.js
Foundation Board of Directors.

00:29:51.690 --> 00:29:53.460
So at NodeSource, we know Node.

00:29:53.460 --> 00:29:54.070
We love it.

00:29:54.070 --> 00:29:55.403
We love the open source project.

00:29:55.403 --> 00:29:58.130
But we also really know
Node in the enterprise.

00:29:58.130 --> 00:29:59.570
Here's a handful
of our customers

00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:01.300
that we've worked with
in the past, where

00:30:01.300 --> 00:30:05.530
we've seen Node at scale
over the past few years.

00:30:05.530 --> 00:30:08.940
And a lot of folks, some
on this slide, for example,

00:30:08.940 --> 00:30:12.405
have had challenges
that they needed met.

00:30:12.405 --> 00:30:14.280
But even with these
folks on this slide,

00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:16.660
I still get this question a lot.

00:30:16.660 --> 00:30:19.400
Is Node really ready
for the enterprise?

00:30:19.400 --> 00:30:21.160
Now, I might be a
little bit biased

00:30:21.160 --> 00:30:25.330
because I have an enterprise
Node.js company that I work at.

00:30:25.330 --> 00:30:29.050
But I think the answer
really is, in fact, yes.

00:30:29.050 --> 00:30:30.170
We are ready.

00:30:30.170 --> 00:30:33.970
There are hundreds,
if not thousands,

00:30:33.970 --> 00:30:35.690
of enterprises
out there actually

00:30:35.690 --> 00:30:39.170
using Node, from folks
like Intuit to Netflix

00:30:39.170 --> 00:30:44.460
to Apple, BMW, Capital
One, NASA, et cetera.

00:30:44.460 --> 00:30:46.860
It's actually more
difficult to find

00:30:46.860 --> 00:30:49.320
a company in the
Global 2000 that's not

00:30:49.320 --> 00:30:51.870
using Node in some capacity.

00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:54.930
So we believe, and we've
seen it empirically,

00:30:54.930 --> 00:30:57.540
that Node is actually really
ready for the enterprise.

00:30:57.540 --> 00:31:00.252
The thing, though, about an open
source project, or even Node

00:31:00.252 --> 00:31:02.460
specifically, is when you
get it into the enterprise,

00:31:02.460 --> 00:31:05.990
there are some challenges
that face its adoption

00:31:05.990 --> 00:31:07.390
and integration.

00:31:07.390 --> 00:31:09.950
And the obvious ones are
things around security.

00:31:09.950 --> 00:31:10.740
Is Node secure?

00:31:10.740 --> 00:31:11.410
Of course it is.

00:31:11.410 --> 00:31:13.790
But there are some
additional demands

00:31:13.790 --> 00:31:16.070
brought by certain
industries around how

00:31:16.070 --> 00:31:17.510
to harden that runtime.

00:31:17.510 --> 00:31:19.840
Who do we call if
something's broken?

00:31:19.840 --> 00:31:23.660
Who's the entity backing
this open source project?

00:31:23.660 --> 00:31:25.730
And then more
specifically with Node,

00:31:25.730 --> 00:31:28.900
how do we get better control
over the runtime and better

00:31:28.900 --> 00:31:32.970
insight into things like
finding performance bottlenecks

00:31:32.970 --> 00:31:35.030
and debugging the application?

00:31:35.030 --> 00:31:38.360
Well, this is precisely why
we built our product N Solid.

00:31:38.360 --> 00:31:43.070
N Solid is, in fact, the
enterprise grade Node.js

00:31:43.070 --> 00:31:43.950
platform.

00:31:43.950 --> 00:31:46.600
And to be clear, it's
not a fork of Node.

00:31:46.600 --> 00:31:50.270
It actually is Node.js with an
additional set of capabilities

00:31:50.270 --> 00:31:53.180
wrapped around it so it
doesn't break interoperability.

00:31:53.180 --> 00:31:55.700
In fact, you don't even have
to touch your application

00:31:55.700 --> 00:31:56.940
code whatsoever.

00:31:56.940 --> 00:32:00.230
And the capabilities that N
Solid actually provides-- well,

00:32:00.230 --> 00:32:02.350
they're the things that
the enterprises told us

00:32:02.350 --> 00:32:03.680
they really need.

00:32:03.680 --> 00:32:06.500
Things like enhanced
security, guard rails,

00:32:06.500 --> 00:32:09.900
production monitoring
of these processes,

00:32:09.900 --> 00:32:12.450
turnkey performance analysis.

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:14.980
We don't want to have to use
a bunch of system tools just

00:32:14.980 --> 00:32:16.410
to profile this.

00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:19.230
How do we get real time
performance analysis?

00:32:19.230 --> 00:32:22.410
Of course, I mentioned things
like support, that stability

00:32:22.410 --> 00:32:23.530
from a commercial vendor.

00:32:23.530 --> 00:32:25.890
And I think one of the
best parts, as a developer,

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:28.156
and how we architected
this early on,

00:32:28.156 --> 00:32:30.610
is there's no code
modification required.

00:32:30.610 --> 00:32:33.720
So the application that you've
currently written in Node.js

00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:35.910
will work with N
Solid by default.

00:32:35.910 --> 00:32:36.950
And I'm really pleased.

00:32:36.950 --> 00:32:39.960
Earlier this week, we actually
announced our partnership

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:41.140
with Google Cloud.

00:32:41.140 --> 00:32:44.280
So N Solid is, in fact,
available on Google Cloud.

00:32:44.280 --> 00:32:46.735
So let me show you
a couple of demos.

00:32:49.420 --> 00:32:54.920
So let's change our project
to NodeSource first.

00:32:54.920 --> 00:32:58.950
And we are actually
running on Compute Engine.

00:32:58.950 --> 00:33:00.950
And so if you look
here, what you'll see

00:33:00.950 --> 00:33:06.520
is a handful of VMs with some
various Node.js applications.

00:33:06.520 --> 00:33:08.480
Now, in production,
you wouldn't do this.

00:33:08.480 --> 00:33:11.270
You would actually run an
autoscaling managed instance

00:33:11.270 --> 00:33:13.020
group as opposed to
just a handful of VMs.

00:33:13.020 --> 00:33:16.275
But it's a demo, so we
can deal with this now.

00:33:16.275 --> 00:33:18.750
What you see here is
a handful of apps.

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:21.340
And this one in particular,
this is Dillinger.

00:33:21.340 --> 00:33:22.930
This is actually a
real application.

00:33:22.930 --> 00:33:25.360
It's an online markdown editor.

00:33:25.360 --> 00:33:29.450
And we see here there's some
interesting CPU utilization.

00:33:29.450 --> 00:33:31.130
It's kind of hovering
a bit high there,

00:33:31.130 --> 00:33:33.130
which is odd for a
Node.js application.

00:33:33.130 --> 00:33:35.110
Node.js has a low
memory footprint

00:33:35.110 --> 00:33:37.575
and typically has a
low CPU utilization.

00:33:37.575 --> 00:33:40.680
So something
interesting is going on.

00:33:40.680 --> 00:33:42.780
In the Google Cloud
Developer console,

00:33:42.780 --> 00:33:44.670
we can see it from a high level.

00:33:44.670 --> 00:33:47.510
But what N Solid does is it
provides us deeper insight

00:33:47.510 --> 00:33:49.670
from a bottoms-up approach.

00:33:49.670 --> 00:33:51.770
So just so that
you believe me, let

00:33:51.770 --> 00:33:54.570
me show you that Dillinger
is, in fact, a real app.

00:33:54.570 --> 00:33:56.080
So we click on the URL.

00:33:56.080 --> 00:33:57.260
There it is running.

00:33:57.260 --> 00:34:00.420
Yes, it is, in fact, a
real markdown editor.

00:34:00.420 --> 00:34:05.090
And then, if we want to go
dive in deeper into Dillinger

00:34:05.090 --> 00:34:07.730
to kind of triage what the issue
is-- why is that CPU running

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:11.520
so high-- we're actually going
to launch into the N Solid

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:12.480
console.

00:34:12.480 --> 00:34:15.550
So all those applications
that I showed previously

00:34:15.550 --> 00:34:19.340
on the Developer
console for Google Cloud

00:34:19.340 --> 00:34:21.947
are all right here in the
N Solid console as well.

00:34:21.947 --> 00:34:23.530
And lo and behold,
we're going to dive

00:34:23.530 --> 00:34:25.190
in deeper into Dillinger.

00:34:25.190 --> 00:34:26.989
And we can see very
quickly that there

00:34:26.989 --> 00:34:30.000
are a couple processes that
are kind of scattered out there

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:30.942
towards the right.

00:34:30.942 --> 00:34:33.150
Now, it might be a little
difficult to see from here,

00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:35.595
but the x-axis is
measuring CPU utilization

00:34:35.595 --> 00:34:37.590
and the y-axis is memory.

00:34:37.590 --> 00:34:39.170
And what we've
noticed from the folks

00:34:39.170 --> 00:34:41.170
that we've worked with
in the past at NodeSource

00:34:41.170 --> 00:34:44.760
is that the two major
key issues that people

00:34:44.760 --> 00:34:47.960
have with Node.js
development is memory leaks

00:34:47.960 --> 00:34:50.969
and determining where our
bottlenecks are in our code.

00:34:50.969 --> 00:34:53.050
So we already can
see quickly there

00:34:53.050 --> 00:34:56.784
is something kind of going
wrong or interesting-- something

00:34:56.784 --> 00:34:58.450
worth inspecting, I
guess I should say--

00:34:58.450 --> 00:35:00.110
with this particular process.

00:35:00.110 --> 00:35:03.800
So if we look, we get a little
bit more detailed view here.

00:35:03.800 --> 00:35:05.460
But it's not really
telling us much

00:35:05.460 --> 00:35:07.590
because there's not any
load actually hitting it.

00:35:07.590 --> 00:35:11.920
So why don't we go ahead
and trigger some load

00:35:11.920 --> 00:35:13.860
with ApacheBench.

00:35:13.860 --> 00:35:16.130
This is an open source
load testing tool.

00:35:16.130 --> 00:35:19.120
Actually, Google has a really
awesome distributed load

00:35:19.120 --> 00:35:20.000
testing tool.

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:22.920
But just for ease
of use, we'll just

00:35:22.920 --> 00:35:24.730
go with ApacheBench for now.

00:35:24.730 --> 00:35:26.800
So given the
latency of the Wi-Fi

00:35:26.800 --> 00:35:29.960
and anything else, what we
should see in near real time

00:35:29.960 --> 00:35:34.770
is these processes actually
reacting to the traffic.

00:35:34.770 --> 00:35:37.350
And what we want to do, with
a few clicks of a button,

00:35:37.350 --> 00:35:40.140
is actually profile
this application

00:35:40.140 --> 00:35:42.240
and generate what's
called a flame graph.

00:35:42.240 --> 00:35:45.140
And if you've never generated
a flame graph, it's OK.

00:35:45.140 --> 00:35:47.860
Basically, what you're looking
for in a flame graph is

00:35:47.860 --> 00:35:50.480
the width of these
function calls

00:35:50.480 --> 00:35:52.590
and how long they're
sitting on CPU.

00:35:52.590 --> 00:35:54.360
And what we can see
very quickly here

00:35:54.360 --> 00:35:56.690
is this average time function.

00:35:56.690 --> 00:36:03.020
It's taking up 77% of
the CPU utilization

00:36:03.020 --> 00:36:05.010
for this particular process.

00:36:05.010 --> 00:36:08.590
Now, what we're doing
here is somewhat magical,

00:36:08.590 --> 00:36:12.040
but it's meant to actually help
drive towards issue resolution

00:36:12.040 --> 00:36:12.980
faster.

00:36:12.980 --> 00:36:16.370
We can see from this line
here, this is actually

00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:18.670
where that function is defined.

00:36:18.670 --> 00:36:20.750
And we can immediately
go and figure out

00:36:20.750 --> 00:36:23.620
what went wrong with the
writing of that function

00:36:23.620 --> 00:36:25.220
to make it so slow.

00:36:25.220 --> 00:36:28.450
This is only a snapshot
of the capabilities

00:36:28.450 --> 00:36:29.592
that N Solid provides.

00:36:29.592 --> 00:36:30.800
We do have a booth out front.

00:36:30.800 --> 00:36:32.449
I recommend people
coming by to see

00:36:32.449 --> 00:36:34.740
some of the enhanced security
guard rails that we have,

00:36:34.740 --> 00:36:37.510
show you some of the heat
snapshot capabilities.

00:36:37.510 --> 00:36:39.760
But in the meantime,
I will say I'm really

00:36:39.760 --> 00:36:42.630
excited to be working
with Justin and the team

00:36:42.630 --> 00:36:46.885
because the scale of Google
Cloud and the history

00:36:46.885 --> 00:36:50.560
that they have behind compute
intensive tasks, coupled

00:36:50.560 --> 00:36:54.030
with something like N Solid,
really can help drive home

00:36:54.030 --> 00:36:56.170
Node.js apps in the enterprise.

00:36:56.170 --> 00:36:57.920
Thank you.

00:36:57.920 --> 00:37:00.420
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:00.420 --> 00:37:02.626
JUSTIN BECKWITH: Great job.

00:37:02.626 --> 00:37:04.610
Thank you, Joe.

00:37:04.610 --> 00:37:07.280
So we've done a lot today.

00:37:07.280 --> 00:37:09.092
We run JavaScript on the client.

00:37:09.092 --> 00:37:10.550
We obviously had
a few demos there.

00:37:10.550 --> 00:37:12.060
We ran it on the server.

00:37:12.060 --> 00:37:14.250
That's one of my favorite
things about Node.js

00:37:14.250 --> 00:37:15.850
and about JavaScript
specifically

00:37:15.850 --> 00:37:16.975
is that it runs everywhere.

00:37:16.975 --> 00:37:20.204
We're running it on our phones,
on our desktops, on servers.

00:37:20.204 --> 00:37:21.620
And now we're even
seeing it start

00:37:21.620 --> 00:37:24.600
to run on other
alternative devices.

00:37:24.600 --> 00:37:26.340
One of my favorite
quotes out there

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:28.890
comes from Jeff Atwood
of Stack Overflow frame,

00:37:28.890 --> 00:37:32.280
which is "Anything that can
be written in JavaScript

00:37:32.280 --> 00:37:34.595
will eventually be
written in JavaScript."

00:37:34.595 --> 00:37:36.220
And I think where
we're really starting

00:37:36.220 --> 00:37:40.500
to see that claim come to life
is with the Internet of Things.

00:37:40.500 --> 00:37:42.734
So IoT is exploding right now.

00:37:42.734 --> 00:37:44.900
And one of the top languages
that people are picking

00:37:44.900 --> 00:37:46.630
to choose is JavaScript.

00:37:46.630 --> 00:37:49.220
And a big reason for that
is that it's ubiquitous.

00:37:49.220 --> 00:37:49.990
It's everywhere.

00:37:49.990 --> 00:37:51.260
People already know it.

00:37:51.260 --> 00:37:53.765
All those skills that we've
learned throughout the last 10

00:37:53.765 --> 00:37:56.280
or 15 years building
front end JavaScript apps,

00:37:56.280 --> 00:37:58.980
and now the last six, seven
years building back end apps,

00:37:58.980 --> 00:38:02.270
we can apply to devices
in the cloud as well.

00:38:02.270 --> 00:38:04.870
There are a lot of
great options out there.

00:38:04.870 --> 00:38:06.540
If you have something
like an Arduino,

00:38:06.540 --> 00:38:08.522
or if you have something
like a Raspberry Pi,

00:38:08.522 --> 00:38:10.230
there are some NPM
modules-- Johnny-Five,

00:38:10.230 --> 00:38:14.790
Cylon.js-- that make using these
devices really, really simple.

00:38:14.790 --> 00:38:16.530
And Google is getting
involved here, too.

00:38:16.530 --> 00:38:18.165
We have things like
Brillo and Weave,

00:38:18.165 --> 00:38:20.290
some new protocols and
tools are out there to help,

00:38:20.290 --> 00:38:22.850
and even help connect
them with Android.

00:38:22.850 --> 00:38:24.520
Now, when you do
build something that's

00:38:24.520 --> 00:38:26.020
IoT-based-- let's
say that we go out

00:38:26.020 --> 00:38:27.894
and we have a car or a
washing machine that's

00:38:27.894 --> 00:38:29.310
internet connected--
you're going

00:38:29.310 --> 00:38:30.920
to generate a lot of data.

00:38:30.920 --> 00:38:32.530
BigQuery is a great
tool that we can

00:38:32.530 --> 00:38:34.430
use when it's time to
actually take that data

00:38:34.430 --> 00:38:36.890
and try to gather some
insights out of it.

00:38:36.890 --> 00:38:39.855
But all that sounds
great and exciting.

00:38:39.855 --> 00:38:42.480
What I wanted to do today is we
have a little bit of time left.

00:38:42.480 --> 00:38:43.688
I think we have minutes left.

00:38:43.688 --> 00:38:45.310
I want to have some fun.

00:38:45.310 --> 00:38:47.990
And what I want to do is
I'm going to show a toy.

00:38:47.990 --> 00:38:50.010
Now, these toys
right here-- and they

00:38:50.010 --> 00:38:52.820
may be kind of hard to see--
these are called Little Bits.

00:38:52.820 --> 00:38:55.850
And Little Bits are these tiny,
little, electronic building

00:38:55.850 --> 00:38:57.180
block components.

00:38:57.180 --> 00:38:59.630
They're great for the young
or the young at heart.

00:38:59.630 --> 00:39:02.930
And really, they're about
teaching children electronics

00:39:02.930 --> 00:39:06.610
and about getting excited
about making things.

00:39:06.610 --> 00:39:09.490
I know that's something
that I wanted to share.

00:39:09.490 --> 00:39:11.930
And so the next part
we're going to dig into

00:39:11.930 --> 00:39:15.070
is how we can use Little Bits,
connect them to the internet,

00:39:15.070 --> 00:39:19.050
and then actually control them
with Node.js on Google Cloud.

00:39:19.050 --> 00:39:21.179
So before we dig
into the actual demo,

00:39:21.179 --> 00:39:23.220
I'm going to take a look
at a little bit of code.

00:39:30.090 --> 00:39:30.990
There we go.

00:39:30.990 --> 00:39:33.670
So this is the Hat Spin project.

00:39:33.670 --> 00:39:36.215
I'm going to open up Server.js.

00:39:36.215 --> 00:39:38.050
You can see I'm back
to using Express again.

00:39:38.050 --> 00:39:39.940
I just want to walk through
a few of the NPM modules

00:39:39.940 --> 00:39:42.314
I'm using and talk about how
they're going to communicate

00:39:42.314 --> 00:39:44.100
with these devices.

00:39:44.100 --> 00:39:47.860
So here we have the
Little Bits API.

00:39:47.860 --> 00:39:50.911
And that's using
Little Bits Cloud HTTP.

00:39:50.911 --> 00:39:52.410
And they have an
NPM module that you

00:39:52.410 --> 00:39:54.159
can install that will
actually communicate

00:39:54.159 --> 00:39:55.310
with this thing over cloud.

00:39:55.310 --> 00:39:57.260
Pretty simple to use.

00:39:57.260 --> 00:39:58.860
Again, I'm using
PubNub to connect

00:39:58.860 --> 00:40:00.320
to an internet connected client.

00:40:00.320 --> 00:40:03.670
So we're going to see a web
page here in a little bit.

00:40:03.670 --> 00:40:05.130
In this case, I
happen to be using

00:40:05.130 --> 00:40:07.080
Redis to store a few results.

00:40:07.080 --> 00:40:08.740
So you could use Google Cloud.

00:40:08.740 --> 00:40:09.885
You could use Memcache.

00:40:09.885 --> 00:40:12.260
But if you want to come use
Redis, that's perfectly fine.

00:40:12.260 --> 00:40:14.170
I'm using Redis
Labs as an example

00:40:14.170 --> 00:40:16.825
right here just because
it was easy to set up.

00:40:16.825 --> 00:40:18.950
And then let's get down to
the part that's actually

00:40:18.950 --> 00:40:20.930
doing something interesting.

00:40:20.930 --> 00:40:22.830
When you guys click
on a button in a link

00:40:22.830 --> 00:40:24.255
that I'm going to send
you out in a little bit--

00:40:24.255 --> 00:40:25.870
I'm having a little bit of
a problem with my device.

00:40:25.870 --> 00:40:27.078
I have to reset it over here.

00:40:27.078 --> 00:40:29.210
Give me one second.

00:40:29.210 --> 00:40:31.870
Make sure that my phone is
serving everything the way

00:40:31.870 --> 00:40:34.240
it's supposed to.

00:40:34.240 --> 00:40:35.180
All right.

00:40:35.180 --> 00:40:38.110
Sorry for the interruption.

00:40:38.110 --> 00:40:39.570
And so with a
little bit of luck,

00:40:39.570 --> 00:40:41.680
assuming the technology
doesn't fail us today--

00:40:41.680 --> 00:40:45.150
this is all very new-- every
time we come in here and click,

00:40:45.150 --> 00:40:46.100
we're going to count.

00:40:46.100 --> 00:40:48.490
We're going to try to
get up to 100 clicks.

00:40:48.490 --> 00:40:50.230
And once we get to
100, we're going

00:40:50.230 --> 00:40:54.200
to send a 20-second high
edge signal to a device.

00:40:54.200 --> 00:40:56.764
So let's take a look
at what type of device

00:40:56.764 --> 00:40:57.555
we're going to use.

00:40:59.971 --> 00:41:01.470
Some of you may
have seen this movie

00:41:01.470 --> 00:41:03.050
where there's a
bunch of guys that

00:41:03.050 --> 00:41:04.494
try to get a job at Google.

00:41:04.494 --> 00:41:05.910
And in the end,
they do get a job.

00:41:05.910 --> 00:41:07.720
And when they're
finished with that,

00:41:07.720 --> 00:41:11.000
they get one of these
fancy hats, right?

00:41:11.000 --> 00:41:13.860
Now, I was very excited
when I got my Noogler hat.

00:41:13.860 --> 00:41:16.380
And the very first thing
I thought to myself

00:41:16.380 --> 00:41:19.070
when I got it was,
you know what?

00:41:19.070 --> 00:41:21.810
I'd better connect this
thing to the internet

00:41:21.810 --> 00:41:25.030
because I'm at Google now
and that's what we do.

00:41:25.030 --> 00:41:26.560
And so-- oh my gosh.

00:41:26.560 --> 00:41:28.620
It's not starting up.

00:41:28.620 --> 00:41:32.120
And so I went and we built this.

00:41:32.120 --> 00:41:33.900
And what I want
everyone to do is

00:41:33.900 --> 00:41:37.490
I want you to go out
to hatspin.net and see

00:41:37.490 --> 00:41:40.690
if we can coax my hat
to wake up and activate.

00:41:40.690 --> 00:41:42.850
Unfortunately, right
now, it's giving me

00:41:42.850 --> 00:41:45.382
a little bit of trouble.

00:41:45.382 --> 00:41:46.840
But I'm going to
hope for the best.

00:41:46.840 --> 00:41:48.631
And worst case, I look
like a fool in front

00:41:48.631 --> 00:41:49.790
of a roomful of people.

00:41:49.790 --> 00:41:50.420
Whatever.

00:41:50.420 --> 00:41:52.700
Wouldn't be the first time.

00:41:52.700 --> 00:41:53.220
All right.

00:41:53.220 --> 00:41:55.370
So I'm running Hat
Spin over here.

00:41:55.370 --> 00:41:59.470
And what I can do is I
can actually watch-- here,

00:41:59.470 --> 00:42:00.390
I'm going to reset it.

00:42:04.780 --> 00:42:05.500
All right.

00:42:05.500 --> 00:42:06.583
If you voted-- oh my gosh.

00:42:06.583 --> 00:42:09.970
You guys are voting so fast.

00:42:09.970 --> 00:42:12.240
[LAUGHING]

00:42:12.240 --> 00:42:12.834
All right.

00:42:15.105 --> 00:42:16.730
Unfortunately, it's
having a little bit

00:42:16.730 --> 00:42:19.600
of trouble with the Wi-Fi, even
though it was working earlier.

00:42:19.600 --> 00:42:21.355
I apologize, folks.

00:42:21.355 --> 00:42:23.230
I'm going to get this
working after the event

00:42:23.230 --> 00:42:24.310
if you want to
come up and see it.

00:42:24.310 --> 00:42:26.226
And we'll give it a try
again in a little bit.

00:42:30.770 --> 00:42:33.140
So we covered a
lot of stuff today.

00:42:33.140 --> 00:42:37.060
We had some ups, we had some
downs, we had some hats.

00:42:37.060 --> 00:42:40.544
App Engine is making it easy
to operate at Google scale.

00:42:40.544 --> 00:42:42.460
So the applications that
you're writing today,

00:42:42.460 --> 00:42:43.710
we want to make it
as simple as possible

00:42:43.710 --> 00:42:45.510
for you to focus on
building the app,

00:42:45.510 --> 00:42:49.030
and not having to worry
about the infrastructure.

00:42:49.030 --> 00:42:50.500
You can use the
tools and libraries

00:42:50.500 --> 00:42:52.625
you're already using, use
the things that you know,

00:42:52.625 --> 00:42:54.069
the things that you love.

00:42:54.069 --> 00:42:56.110
When you are ready, if
you want to take advantage

00:42:56.110 --> 00:42:58.100
of our amazing
APIs and services,

00:42:58.100 --> 00:42:59.140
that's available to you.

00:42:59.140 --> 00:43:01.870
And that's really the magic
that's behind Google Cloud.

00:43:01.870 --> 00:43:05.300
And finally, the big thing is
that Node.js and Google Cloud

00:43:05.300 --> 00:43:07.270
together, we're all
ready for the enterprise.

00:43:07.270 --> 00:43:09.340
We're ready for serious
applications, not just

00:43:09.340 --> 00:43:10.780
hats and cats.

00:43:10.780 --> 00:43:12.590
But we're ready for
the real applications

00:43:12.590 --> 00:43:15.420
that are needed out there
today to change the world.

00:43:15.420 --> 00:43:17.290
Everything we looked
at today, end to end,

00:43:17.290 --> 00:43:19.910
is open source-- the
Docker images we're using,

00:43:19.910 --> 00:43:24.180
the GCloud NPM library, Trace
and Debugger tools, the API

00:43:24.180 --> 00:43:25.369
client, all the samples.

00:43:25.369 --> 00:43:27.660
